#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
dcg-ctx-wrap — Claude Code PreToolUse adapter for context-mode ctx_execute.

context-mode's ctx_execute tool runs arbitrary shell/python/js/etc code in a
sandbox. The DCG hook is wired only for Bash, so ctx_execute became a wide-open
emission channel and let `infisical secrets --plain` through on 2026-05-15.

This adapter:
1. Reads Claude Code hook JSON from stdin.
2. If tool_name CONTAINS one of CTX_TOOL_TOKENS, extracts every code-bearing field
   and rewrites the payload as a synthetic Bash call so dcg's existing pattern
   packs run against the code text verbatim.
3. Pipes the rewritten payload to dcg-wrap, which runs dcg + Discord alerting.
4. Emits dcg-wrap's decision back to Claude Code unchanged.

Tool-name matching is an UNANCHORED SUBSTRING match, not exact membership and not
endswith (transformate WI-2096): the harness renamed the tools from
`mcp__context-mode__ctx_execute` to `mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute`,
and the exact-membership set silently fell through to fail_open() — a live guardrail
bypass for months. A substring match survives the next prefix change instead of failing
open, and unlike endswith it also covers the decorated names the unanchored settings
matcher really does route here. The constant used to be named for suffixes; that name
asserted a right-anchoring the code deliberately does not have, and that reading has
already caused one reintroduction of the bypass, so it is now CTX_TOOL_TOKENS.

For non-shell languages (javascript/python/etc), dcg's regex patterns scan
the raw source — substrings like `infisical secrets --plain` will match
whether they appear in `bash` or in a JS `execSync(...)` string. This is
intentional: defends against the same leak vector in any wrapping language.

DECISION CONTRACT — which way this adapter fails, and why it changed.

The rule is: **once a call is identified as a guarded context-mode tool, it is
never allowed through on the strength of an adapter or scanner failure.**

  not our surface          -> ALLOW
    * stdin is not parseable JSON AND its raw bytes name no guarded tool. We
      cannot identify the call and nothing claims we should. The harness writes
      this stdin; malformed input means the harness is broken, not that a caller
      evaded us.
    * tool_name is a string naming something other than a context-mode exec tool.
    * the payload carried strings and every one of them is blank — there is
      genuinely no code text to scan.
    * THE SCANNER RAN AND SAID NOTHING: exit 0 with empty stdout. That is how dcg
      spells "allow" — it emits an envelope only when it objects. Measured, not
      assumed; reading it as "no decision" denied every benign guarded call.

  unidentifiable, but the raw bytes name a guarded tool  -> DENY
    * stdin does not parse at all.
    * the top-level document is not a JSON object.
    * tool_name is missing, empty, or not a string.

  guarded, but we cannot render a verdict  -> DENY
    * tool_input is not an object.
    * the payload carries no string fields at all: an unrecognized shape, which
      is exactly what a schema rename looks like from in here.
    * dcg-wrap cannot be invoked, times out, or dies.
    * dcg-wrap exits NON-ZERO having printed nothing — a crash, not a clean scan.
    * dcg-wrap prints something that is not a recognisable decision, at any exit.
    * any unexpected exception after the tool was identified as guarded.

This REPLACES the previous blanket fail-open. That guarantee read "the adapter
must never block real work because of its own bugs", which is a good principle
applied to the wrong set: a missing scanner binary is not the adapter's bug, and
once `is_ctx_tool()` is true this adapter is the ONLY path from the ctx tools
into dcg — the Bash matcher does not fire for them. So an operational failure of
the scanner silently reopened precisely the channel that leaked on 2026-05-15.
Measured, not theorised: `DCG_WRAP_BIN=/nonexistent` previously emitted
`{"continue": true}` and exit 0.

IDENTIFICATION IS PART OF THAT CONTRACT.

Every fail-closed rule above begins "once a call is IDENTIFIED as guarded". So an
attacker never had to beat them — only to stop them applying. Two routes did
exactly that, and both returned `{"continue": true}` with the scanner NEVER
invoked, through the one path this contract blesses as ALLOW:

  * `json.loads()` RECURSES. Nesting any field deep enough raises RecursionError,
    which is an Exception, which the parse guard caught and turned into allow().
    MAX_DEPTH does not help — it applies inside collect_strings, AFTER the parse.
    Measured against this file's parent commit, a guarded ctx_execute carrying a
    deep sibling field, scanner never run:
        python 3.11.15 -> allowed from depth ~1100
        python 3.12.3  -> allowed from depth ~10000   (TEAM-1's interpreter)
        python 3.14.3  -> allowed from depth ~20000
    The interpreter sets the price, not whether the door is open.
  * a NON-STRING `tool_name` reached the same allow() via the old
    `not isinstance(tool, str)` arm — on every interpreter, at any payload size,
    and reachable without any adversary at all.

The close: an identification failure is "not our surface" only if nothing in the
raw stdin claims otherwise. The harness serialises tool_name with a standard JSON
encoder, which leaves ASCII literal, so a guarded token is present VERBATIM in the
bytes even when the document as a whole will not parse. If it is there, refuse.
This is consulted ONLY on identification failure; it never overrides a successful
identification, so a Bash call whose command text merely mentions a ctx tool name
is still allowed by the normal path.

Deliberately NOT done: scanning the raw text for nesting depth so the parse is
never attempted at all. That would additionally close a parser CRASH, since a dead
hook emits no denial — but no crash is reachable: json.loads raised RecursionError
cleanly at 2e5, 1e6 and 5e6 levels on 3.12.3. Buying an undemonstrable case with a
hand-rolled string-aware bracket scanner inside the control is a bad trade.

The field-name keying is gone for the same reason the tool-name keying went in
transformate WI-2096. Keying on `code` / `commands[].command` had identical drift
semantics one layer down: rename the field and a guarded tool yields "no code"
and sails through, with the suite green. Extraction now walks the whole
tool_input and scans EVERY string it finds, so a renamed field is still read.
Over-matching is deliberate and is not a defect: dcg scans text, and a path or a
label that happens to be scanned costs nothing.

Tracked under: transformate WI-117 SEC-V1 Phase 1; fail-closed contract and
shape-drift extraction under transformate WI-2096.
"""

import json
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys

DCG_WRAP = os.environ.get(
    "DCG_WRAP_BIN",
    os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~"), ".local/bin/dcg-wrap"),
)
# Substring match: covers the legacy `mcp__context-mode__*` names AND the live
# plugin-prefixed `mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__*` names, and any
# future prefix. ctx_execute is a prefix of ctx_execute_file, so both are hit
# by the first entry — listed separately for grep-ability.
CTX_TOOL_TOKENS = (
    "context-mode__ctx_execute",
    "context-mode__ctx_execute_file",
    "context-mode__ctx_batch_execute",
)

# Guard against a pathologically nested payload turning extraction into a hang.
# Real ctx payloads are 2-3 levels deep (tool_input -> commands[] -> {command}).
MAX_DEPTH = 12


def allow():
    """Emit a continue:true decision so Claude Code proceeds."""
    print(json.dumps({"continue": True}))
    sys.exit(0)


def deny(reason):
    """Emit a deny decision. Used whenever a GUARDED call cannot be verdicted."""
    print(json.dumps({
        "hookSpecificOutput": {
            "hookEventName": "PreToolUse",
            "permissionDecision": "deny",
            "permissionDecisionReason": "dcg-ctx-wrap: " + reason,
        }
    }))
    sys.exit(0)


class ShapeTooDeep(Exception):
    """The payload nests deeper than we are willing to walk."""


def is_hook_decision(obj):
    """Is this actually a Claude Code hook decision, rather than merely valid JSON?

    "Parses as a JSON object" is not the same as "carries a verdict". `dcg-wrap` emitting a
    diagnostic envelope — `{"error": {...}}`, or a bare `{}` — used to be forwarded verbatim,
    and because it contains no denial the host reads it as no objection. That is the fail-open
    this adapter exists to close, arriving as well-formed JSON.
    """
    if not isinstance(obj, dict):
        return False
    if isinstance(obj.get("continue"), bool):
        return True
    hso = obj.get("hookSpecificOutput")
    if isinstance(hso, dict):
        # hookEventName is required, not decorative: the host keys on it, so an envelope
        # without it may be ignored — and an ignored DENY is an allow. Safe to demand,
        # measured: the real dcg emits hookEventName "PreToolUse" on every deny envelope.
        if (hso.get("hookEventName") == "PreToolUse"
                and hso.get("permissionDecision") in ("allow", "deny", "ask")):
            return True
    return False


_U_ESCAPE = re.compile(r"\\u([0-9a-fA-F]{4})")


def raw_carries_guarded_token(raw):
    """Do the RAW stdin bytes name a guarded context-mode tool?

    Consulted ONLY when identification failed — never as an override of a successful one.
    A Bash call whose command text merely mentions a ctx tool name parses fine, identifies
    as Bash and is allowed by the normal path; this function never sees it.
    """
    if not isinstance(raw, str) or not raw:
        return False
    if any(s in raw for s in CTX_TOOL_TOKENS):
        return True
    # A hand-written document could \u-escape the token to hide it from the literal scan.
    # Decoding is a linear regex pass with no recursion, which also makes it safe to run on
    # the path where a RecursionError has just unwound.
    if "\\u" in raw:
        try:
            decoded = _U_ESCAPE.sub(lambda m: chr(int(m.group(1), 16)), raw)
        except Exception:
            return False
        return any(s in decoded for s in CTX_TOOL_TOKENS)
    return False


def unidentified(raw, why):
    """Terminal decision for a call this adapter could not identify.

    Identification failure is the cheapest way past a fail-closed contract, because every
    rule in that contract is conditioned on the call having been identified first. So an
    unidentifiable call is "not our surface" only while nothing in the bytes says otherwise.
    """
    if raw_carries_guarded_token(raw):
        deny("%s, and stdin names a guarded context-mode tool — refusing a call that "
             "cannot be identified" % why)
    allow()


def is_ctx_tool(tool):
    # SUBSTRING membership, deliberately — NOT `tool.endswith(...)`.
    #
    # `endswith` was applied once and reverted, because it is a fail-open of exactly the
    # WI-2096 shape. The settings.json matcher that routes calls here is UNANCHORED, so the
    # harness sends names like `…__ctx_execute_v2` to this adapter — and under `endswith`
    # they fall straight through to `allow()` with the scanner never invoked, while the host
    # still believes the surface is guarded. Measured on all three of `…__ctx_execute_v2`,
    # `…__ctx_execute2` and `…__ctx_batch_execute_v2`.
    #
    # The cost of substring matching is OVER-matching: a name that merely contains a guarded
    # token gets scanned. That is the intended trade — dcg scans text, so a needless scan
    # costs nothing, while a missed one is the incident this file exists to prevent.
    return any(s in tool for s in CTX_TOOL_TOKENS)


def collect_strings(value, depth=0):
    """Every string anywhere in the tool input, in document order.

    Deliberately shape-agnostic: it does NOT key on field names. Keying on
    `code` / `commands[].command` reproduced the WI-2096 drift one layer down —
    rename the field and a guarded call extracts nothing and fails open. Walking
    the structure means a renamed field is still scanned.

    dcg scans text, so joining everything into one document is equivalent to
    scanning each piece: any single offending fragment still matches.
    """
    if depth > MAX_DEPTH:
        # NOT `return []`. Silently yielding nothing below the ceiling means a payload with a
        # benign shallow label and dangerous code nested deeper gets scanned as the benign
        # fragment alone, and the guarded call proceeds — which is precisely the "every string
        # is scanned" claim failing quietly. Raise, so the guarded path denies.
        raise ShapeTooDeep("payload nests deeper than %d levels" % MAX_DEPTH)
    if isinstance(value, str):
        return [value]
    out = []
    if isinstance(value, dict):
        for item in value.values():
            out.extend(collect_strings(item, depth + 1))
    elif isinstance(value, list):
        for item in value:
            out.extend(collect_strings(item, depth + 1))
    return out


def main():
    # IDENTIFICATION, and it is fail-closed too — see the module docstring. A call we
    # cannot identify is allowed only while the raw bytes make no claim on the guarded
    # surface; `unidentified()` is what enforces that, and it never falls through.
    try:
        raw = sys.stdin.read()
    except Exception:
        # No bytes at all. There is no call here to attribute, guarded or otherwise, and
        # no evidence to weigh either way.
        allow()

    try:
        inp = json.loads(raw)
    except Exception as exc:
        # Catches RecursionError from a deeply nested payload, which was the cheapest
        # route past this adapter, as well as ordinary malformed input.
        unidentified(raw, "stdin did not parse as JSON (%s)" % type(exc).__name__)
    if not isinstance(inp, dict):
        unidentified(raw, "stdin parsed to %s, not a hook object" % type(inp).__name__)

    # `toolName` is consulted only when `tool_name` is ABSENT. A present-but-wrong-typed
    # tool_name is a corrupt claim about the call, not an absence of one, so it must not be
    # possible to park a guarded name in `tool_name` and a benign one in `toolName`.
    tool = inp.get("tool_name")
    if tool is None:
        tool = inp.get("toolName")
    if not isinstance(tool, str) or not tool:
        unidentified(raw, "tool_name is %s, not a usable tool name" % type(tool).__name__)
    if not is_ctx_tool(tool):
        # Identification SUCCEEDED and the answer is "not ours". The raw-bytes check is
        # deliberately NOT applied here: it is a tie-breaker for unidentifiable input, not
        # a second opinion about a call we read correctly.
        allow()

    # ---- from here the call is GUARDED: no path out of this function allows it
    # through because something failed. ----
    ti = inp.get("tool_input")
    if ti is None:
        ti = inp.get("toolInput")
    if ti is None:
        # A guarded call with NO payload at all is a truncated or malformed invocation, not a
        # deliberate no-op. Defaulting it to {} made those two indistinguishable and then let
        # the empty case through.
        deny("guarded tool %s carried no tool_input at all" % tool)
    if not isinstance(ti, dict):
        deny("guarded tool %s carried a non-object tool_input (%s)" % (tool, type(ti).__name__))
    if not ti:
        deny("guarded tool %s carried an empty tool_input" % tool)

    try:
        strings = collect_strings(ti)
    except ShapeTooDeep as exc:
        deny("guarded tool %s: %s — refusing rather than scanning part of it" % (tool, exc))
    if not strings:
        # Strings are the only thing this adapter can hand to the scanner. A payload with none
        # anywhere is a shape it does not understand, which is exactly what a field rename
        # looks like from in here.
        deny(
            "guarded tool %s carried no string fields — unrecognized payload shape, "
            "refusing rather than scanning nothing" % tool
        )

    code = "\n".join(s for s in strings if s.strip())
    if not code:
        # Strings were present and every one was blank: genuinely nothing to scan.
        allow()

    language = ti.get("language")
    synthetic = {
        "tool_name": "Bash",
        "tool_input": {
            "command": code,
            "_dcg_source_tool": tool,
            "_dcg_source_language": language if isinstance(language, str) else "",
        },
    }

    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            [DCG_WRAP],
            input=json.dumps(synthetic),
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
            timeout=8,
        )
    except Exception as exc:
        # Missing binary, timeout, permission error. This adapter is the ONLY
        # path from the ctx tools into dcg, so allowing here reopens the channel.
        deny("scanner %s could not be run (%s: %s)" % (DCG_WRAP, type(exc).__name__, exc))

    # HOW THE REAL SCANNER SPEAKS. Measured against dcg on TEAM-1, not inferred from the
    # test stub:
    #     allow -> exit 0 and NOTHING on stdout. Silence IS the allow.
    #     deny  -> a full hookSpecificOutput envelope, also exit 0. Never exit 2.
    # So "produced no parseable decision" cannot mean DENY on its own. It did, and that
    # denied every BENIGN guarded call: a total outage of all three ctx tools on any host
    # running the real dcg. The suite stayed green throughout because the stub always
    # prints a decision, which is a thing dcg only does when it objects.
    stdout = proc.stdout or ""
    decision = None
    if stdout.strip():
        try:
            decision = json.loads(stdout)
        except ValueError:
            decision = None

    if decision is None and not stdout.strip():
        # Silence. Separate "scanned it and objected to nothing" from "never ran, or died
        # mid-scan": the first exits 0, the second does not. A binary that cannot be
        # executed at all never reaches here — subprocess raises and the handler above
        # denies. RESIDUAL, stated because it cannot be closed from in here: a scanner that
        # exits 0 having done nothing (a stub, a `true`, a shell wrapper whose exec failed
        # silently) is indistinguishable on the wire from a clean scan. Catching that is
        # install-time work — apply-wi2096-matcher.mjs canaries a known-dangerous fixture
        # and requires the DENY to have come from the scanner rather than from this file.
        if proc.returncode == 0:
            allow()
        deny("scanner exited %s with no output — a clean scan and a crash are not the "
             "same thing" % proc.returncode)

    if not is_hook_decision(decision):
        sys.stderr.write(proc.stderr or "")
        deny(
            "scanner returned no recognisable decision (exit %s, %d bytes on stdout)"
            % (proc.returncode, len(stdout))
        )

    # A recognised decision passes through untouched, but the exit status is NORMALISED.
    # The host reads 2 as a blocking error and every OTHER non-zero as a non-blocking one,
    # after which the tool proceeds — so forwarding a deny that happened to exit 3 would
    # print the denial and run the call anyway. dcg itself denies with exit 0, so this is
    # defence against a future scanner, not a description of the current one.
    sys.stdout.write(stdout)
    sys.stderr.write(proc.stderr or "")
    sys.exit(2 if proc.returncode == 2 else 0)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        main()
    except SystemExit:
        raise
    except Exception as exc:
        # Reached only if something above raised outside its own handler. We do
        # not know whether the call was guarded, but the only calls that get this
        # far have already passed the is_ctx_tool() gate, so refuse.
        print(json.dumps({
            "hookSpecificOutput": {
                "hookEventName": "PreToolUse",
                "permissionDecision": "deny",
                "permissionDecisionReason":
                    "dcg-ctx-wrap: unhandled %s — refusing a guarded call it could not verdict"
                    % type(exc).__name__,
            }
        }))
        sys.exit(0)
